   "failure_scenario": "When the change runs on the Linux CI runner, Bun names the cut-off reply body with a different transport error than macOS does, so the new truncated-body test fails even though the wrapper behaves correctly: it waits out its budget, exits 75 and never starts the binary. The pull request then cannot land until someone removes the macOS-only wording check.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: the failure needs Bun 1.4.2 on Linux (the CI pin). No Linux Bun binary or image is available offline, and Docker has no bun image. On macOS (Bun 1.4.3), `bun test test/agent-test.test.ts -t \"truncated body\"` gives 5 pass, 0 fail. That is consistent with the claim, which is about Linux only.",
   "evidence": "At head, test/agent-test.test.ts:927 adds `if (mode === \"truncated body\") expect(stderr).toContain(\"ECONNRESET\");`. Every other assertion in that test checks behaviour: exit 75, the full budget waited, and the marker file absent (the binary never started). This one line checks how the platform names the error. Six lines above, at line 921, the comment at head already says truncated headers surface \"as a transport error on macOS and as a timeout on Linux\", so the test file itself admits that error wording differs by OS. AGENTS.md requires changes to \"Pass on Linux CI as well as macOS\". The reviewer quotes a CI failure in which no warning contained ECONNRESET (\"HTTP: TypeError: Unable to connect\u2026\"). The source change in gate-slot.ts, which rethrows body read errors for token-bearing requests, is not at fault."
  },
  "B": {
